= Instructor
Hi! I am Zuriah Quinton, I am a graduate student in the math department. I love teaching, reading, and crocheting. This is my first time teaching Math 1226, but I have taught a few other courses before this one. My pronouns are they/them, and you can call me Zuriah, Quinton, Mx. Quinton, Instructor Quinton, etc (I don't have my PhD yet so I am not a Dr, nor am I a professor, nor am I a Ms). I am excited to be teaching this course, and I hope we can all learn a lot together! Please reach out to me before/after class, in office hours, or by email if you need anything.

= Course Content and Prerequisite Information
Topics of study include techniques and applications of integration; numerical integration using the Trapezoidal and Simpson's Rules; improper integrals; sequences and series; power series; and parametric curves and polar coordinates. Software may be used to supplement analytical methods and to facilitate computational and graphical exploration.

Note that the course has a prereq of Math 1225 (or similar course). This is to ensure students have the proper skills going into this course to support their success. The Department of Mathematics encourages each student enrolled in MATH 1226 to take, at the beginning of the term, an online Basic Skills Review covering basic concepts from MATH 1225. Access to the Basic Skills Review will be available through WebAssign.

The *Basic Skills Review* is a tool you can use to determine if you have been properly placed into this class. Please note that I want everyone to succeed in this class, and if you feel you are missing some of these fundamentals I highly encourage you to fill in those gaps with me during student hours.

