= WebAssign
WebAssign assignments will be assigned regularly, and they will typically be due at 11:59pm ET on the class day following the scheduled lecture. Each question will typically have three attempts, however, multiple choice questions may be adjusted according to the number of available responses. The lowest six Webassign scores will be dropped.  For any questions concerning WebAssign access or support, call (toll-free) 800-955-8275.

Please note the WebAssign assignments are worth 3% of the final grade. The purpose of this assignment is not to get everything perfect on the first try, but to get sufficient practice and see a variety of problems. Multiple attempts are encouraged so students can make mistakes and sort out what went wrong. This is an important part of the learning process for math and should not be skipped.

= Grading
The final course grade is based on the following components:

- 20% In-class quizzes and homework
- 3% WebAssign
- 2% Before Class Reading assignments
- 54% Tests (3 tests each 18%)
- 21% Final Exam (10.5% multiple choice, 10.5% free response)

Any homework will be worth no more than 5% of the total grade, if assigned.

A 90\% will guarantee an A-, 80\% a B-, 70\% a C-, and a 60\% a D-.

= Make-up Policy
Except in cases that are serious, unavoidable, and beyond the student's control few assignments can be made-up. Note 6 WebAssign assignments and 5 in-class quizzes are to be dropped at the end of the semester to accommodate most situations. If you are missing more assignments/classes than this please email the instructor and/or the Dean of Students to determine the best course of action in such cases.

= Grading Appeals
Specific grading disputes (points totaled incorrectly, partial credit appeals, missing assignments on Canvas, etc.) should be brought to the instructor's attention within *3 days* of return of the assignment.  Coming to me within this period does not guarantee a grade change, only my consideration. Appeals outside this timeframe will not be considered.

